{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"For a U.S. ’40 Act ETF, the core arrangements generally include:\n\n- **Fund governance:** A registered investment company (often a series trust), its board of trustees/directors, an investment adviser, and any sub-adviser(s). The adviser oversees portfolio management, compliance, and operations; functions may be performed in-house or delegated.\n- **Fund administration and accounting:** NAV and financial reporting, books and records, tax, and regulatory filings. These functions may be bundled with the adviser, administrator, or custodian, subject to oversight and controls.\n- **Custody and asset servicing:** A qualified custodian holds portfolio assets and handles settlement and income/corporate actions. The custodian may also provide accounting or administration.\n- **Distribution and transfer agency:** A distributor/underwriter supports ETF share distribution; a transfer agent maintains shareholder records and processes creations/redemptions. One provider may serve multiple roles where permitted.\n- **ETF market plumbing:** Authorized Participant(s) transact directly with the fund in creation units; a listing exchange lists and surveils shares; a market maker supports secondary-market liquidity. A clearing agency and settlement system (typically NSCC/DTC) support ETF order processing, clearing, and book-entry settlement. APs and market makers are not necessarily the same firm.\n- **Conditional arrangements:** An index provider and index-licensing agreement are needed for an index-tracking strategy, not an active ETF. Securities-lending agent/program and lending-related indemnification are relevant only if the fund lends securities."}}
